<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Laupen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1339">1339</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Laupen was fought on 21 June 1339 near Laupen, west of Bern, between Bern, supported by troops from the Forest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Cantons</a>, and a coalition of regional nobles allied with the city of Fribourg. Bern's victory secured its expansion and drew it closer to the Swiss Confederacy, which it joined in 1353.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-laupen.jpg" alt="Battle of Laupen" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diebold Schilling the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Laupen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The free imperial city of Bern had expanded at the expense of the feudal lords around it and came into competition with Fribourg, its ally since 1241, which had been sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> in 1277. Fribourg joined a league with the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a>, the County of Savoy, the Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Basel and other lords, raising some 17,000 men, among them about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> under Rudolf von Nidau and Gérard de Valengin, and besieged Laupen Castle. Bern raised 6,000 men, with Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden, allied to it since 1323, and other allies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Bernese arrived on the afternoon of 21 June and drew up on the Bramberg hill, about 3 km from the castle, the Forest Cantons on one flank. Late in the afternoon the Freiburgers attacked. Their cavalry struck the Forest Cantons, who formed an all-round hedgehog, while their infantry climbed the hill. As the Bernese skirmishers fell back, up to 2,000 men at the rear fled into the forest, but the rest held, and the Freiburg infantry broke and fled towards Laupen. Part of the Bernese army then turned to relieve the Forest Cantons and routed the cavalry. At least 80 nobles fell, including the Counts of Nidau, Valengin and Aarburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> force gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a> by the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the Count of Kyburg dispersed on hearing the news. Bern became one of the Eight Cantons of the Confederacy in 1353, and Fribourg, after renewing its alliance with Bern in 1403, joined the Confederacy as an associate by 1454 and in full in 1481. Like Bannockburn 25 years earlier, the battle foreshadowed the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> before infantry. It is also the first battle at which the white cross is documented as a field sign of Swiss combatants, and the first open-field victory of the Confederacy over Habsburg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Laupen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It expanded by buying and conquering lands, defeated a coalition of nobles at <b>Laupen</b> in 1339, and joined the Old Swiss Confederacy in 1353.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-bern">Canton of Bern</a></li></ul></section>
